/*
barcodext.lcb - xTalk Builder (LCB) binding library for zxing-cpp, via the
                barcode_shim C++ shim. Part of barcodext for OpenXTalk (OXT).

Once this extension is loaded, every public handler below is callable
directly from xTalk script, e.g.
    put bcReadText(the imageData of image "photo", the width of image "photo", \
          the height of image "photo") into tText

Conventions (the shim, src/barcode_shim.cpp, is where each is enforced):
  * A PICTURE is the engine's own pixel buffer: an image's imageData (4 bytes
    a pixel) with its width and height. A created barcode comes back as an
    array with "width", "height" and "imageData". Nothing is reordered on the
    way.
  * Nothing here throws, bar the ABI check: a refused call returns empty (an
    empty list or array, empty text) and bcLastError() says why. A picture
    with no barcode in it is not a refusal: the list is empty and
    bcLastError() is empty too.
  * A kind of barcode is named in text ("QR Code", "EAN-13", "Code 128"; the
    spelling is forgiving: "qrcode" and "ean13" name the same kinds), and a
    list of kinds is those names with commas between.
  * A yes/no crosses the public boundary as a Boolean and the C boundary as
    an int (asInt() below; `tR is not 0` on the way back).
  * Bytes cross as a Pointer and a length. An LCB Data does not bridge to a
    void*, so an input is MCDataGetBytePtr + MCDataGetLength and a result is
    copied out of the shim's own buffer with MCDataCreateWithBytes. Text the
    shim RETURNS crosses as a Pointer that textOf() copies, never as
    ZStringNative; a short text ARGUMENT (kinds, options) crosses as
    ZStringUTF8, which the engine builds and frees itself.

The foreign handlers bind to the shared library "barcodext" (built by
CMakeLists.txt). Bindings resolve on first use, so the library only needs to
be present at run time.
*/

/**
Reads QR codes and barcodes out of pictures, and makes them, with zxing-cpp.

It reads and makes the common kinds: QR Code, Data Matrix, Aztec, PDF417,
EAN-13 and EAN-8, UPC-A and UPC-E, Code 128, Code 39, Code 93, ITF, Codabar,
DataBar and more. <bcFormats> lists them.

To read, hand over a picture as an image object's imageData with its width
and height. <bcReadText> returns the text of the first barcode found and
<bcRead> returns every barcode with where it is in the picture. A photo
from a camera works: the barcode may be turned, small, or one of several.

To make one, <bcCreate> returns a picture (an array with "width", "height"
and "imageData") to put into an image object, and <bcCreateSVG> returns the
same barcode as SVG text.

No call here throws on a bad picture or a bad argument. A refused call
returns empty and <bcLastError> says why.

Tags: Barcodes, Images, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.barcodext

use com.livecode.foreign

-- The engine's own builders, resolved by NAME (so no leading underscore).
-- An LCB Data does not bridge to a C pointer: an input is its byte pointer
-- and length, and a result is built from a pointer the shim owns.
foreign handler MCStringCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pByteCount as CUInt, in pEncoding as CUInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
foreign handler MCStringEncode(in pString as String, in pEncoding as CInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
foreign handler MCDataGetBytePtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer binds to "<builtin>"
foreign handler MCDataGetLength(in pData as Data) returns CUInt binds to "<builtin>"
foreign handler MCDataCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pLength as CUInt, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

metadata title is "Barcodes and QR codes for OpenXTalk"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata version is "0.1.0"
metadata summary is "Read QR codes and barcodes out of pictures, and make them, via zxing-cpp."

-- =====================================================================
-- Foreign bindings to the shim (private). One per export of
-- src/barcode_shim.cpp; the suite's tools/check-lcb-signatures.py holds
-- every line to the C definition it binds (return type, each parameter
-- type).
-- =====================================================================

-- ---- the library
private foreign handler _abi_version() returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_abi_version!cdecl"
private foreign handler _library_version() returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_library_version!cdecl"
private foreign handler _last_error() returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_last_error!cdecl"
private foreign handler _text_length(in pText as Pointer) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_text_length!cdecl"
private foreign handler _format_names(in pWritable as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_format_names!cdecl"

-- ---- reading: the results list
private foreign handler _read_image(in pPixels as Pointer, in pLength as CInt, in pWidth as CInt, in pHeight as CInt, in pFormats as ZStringUTF8, in pOptions as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_read_image!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_count() returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_count!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_text(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_text!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_format(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_format!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_content_type(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_content_type!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_symbology_id(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_symbology_id!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_position(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_position!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_extra(in pIndex as CInt, in pKey as ZStringUTF8) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_extra!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_error(in pIndex as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_error!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_bytes(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_bytes!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bytes_pointer() returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_bytes_pointer!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_orientation(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_orientation!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_is_valid(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_is_valid!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_is_mirrored(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_is_mirrored!cdecl"
private foreign handler _result_is_inverted(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_result_is_inverted!cdecl"
private foreign handler _results_release() returns nothing binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_results_release!cdecl"

-- ---- creating: the symbol slot and the render slot
private foreign handler _create_symbol(in pContent as Pointer, in pLength as CInt, in pIsText as CInt, in pFormat as ZStringUTF8, in pOptions as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_create_symbol!cdecl"
private foreign handler _render_image(in pScale as CInt, in pQuietZone as CInt, in pShowText as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_render_image!cdecl"
private foreign handler _render_width() returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_render_width!cdecl"
private foreign handler _render_height() returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_render_height!cdecl"
private foreign handler _render_pixels() returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_render_pixels!cdecl"
private foreign handler _render_pixels_length() returns CInt binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_render_pixels_length!cdecl"
private foreign handler _svg_text(in pScale as CInt, in pQuietZone as CInt, in pShowText as CInt) returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_svg_text!cdecl"
private foreign handler _modules_text() returns Pointer binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_modules_text!cdecl"
private foreign handler _created_release() returns nothing binds to "c:barcodext>bcx_created_release!cdecl"

-- =====================================================================
-- Helpers (private)
-- =====================================================================

private handler asInt(in pFlag as Boolean) returns Integer
   if pFlag then
      return 1
   end if
   return 0
end handler

-- The binding and the native library must come from the same release: a
-- changed export list read through an old binding is a marshalling fault in
-- a call that looks right in both files. The number is BCX_ABI_VERSION in
-- src/barcode_shim.cpp (the suite's check-binary-freshness.py compares the
-- two). Called where a script first reaches the library: every handler that
-- reads or creates, and bcLibraryVersion.
private handler checkABI()
   variable tV as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tV
   end unsafe
   if tV is not 1 then
      throw "barcodext: incompatible native library (this binding needs ABI 1) - update the barcodext library beside the engine"
   end if
end handler

-- A text result of the shim, copied into a String. The shim hands back a
-- pointer to a buffer IT owns, so the foreign handlers return Pointer and
-- never ZStringNative: the engine frees a returned C string, and freeing a
-- static buffer killed box3dxt's first engine run (2026-11-20). The length
-- is the shim's, so a barcode's text that holds a zero byte is not cut
-- short. Encoding 4 is UTF-8.
private handler textOf(in pText as Pointer) returns String
   variable tLength as Integer
   variable tResult as String
   variable tOk as CBool
   unsafe
      put _text_length(pText) into tLength
      put MCStringCreateWithBytes(pText, tLength, 4, false, tResult) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return ""
   end if
   return tResult
end handler

-- How many bytes a Data holds.
private handler lengthOf(in pData as Data) returns Integer
   variable tLength as Integer
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetLength(pData) into tLength
   end unsafe
   return tLength
end handler

-- The bytes of a Data, for the shim to READ. An empty Data has no bytes to
-- point at, so it is given a pointer that is certainly valid (the shim's own
-- error text) beside its length of 0: the shim checks the length before it
-- reads anything, and never reads through a pointer whose length is 0.
private handler pointerOf(in pData as Data) returns Pointer
   variable tPointer as Pointer
   variable tLength as Integer
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetLength(pData) into tLength
      if tLength is 0 then
         put _last_error() into tPointer
      else
         put MCDataGetBytePtr(pData) into tPointer
      end if
   end unsafe
   return tPointer
end handler

-- A copy of pLength bytes the shim owns, as Data.
private handler bytesOf(in pBytes as Pointer, in pLength as Integer) returns Data
   variable tResult as Data
   variable tOk as CBool
   if pLength <= 0 then
      return the empty data
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCDataCreateWithBytes(pBytes, pLength, tResult) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return the empty data
   end if
   return tResult
end handler

-- A String as its UTF-8 bytes (encoding 4), which is what a barcode holds.
private handler utf8Of(in pText as String) returns Data
   variable tData as Data
   variable tOk as CBool
   unsafe
      put MCStringEncode(pText, 4, false, tData) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return the empty data
   end if
   return tData
end handler

-- One barcode of the shim's results list as an array. pIndex is 1-based.
private handler takeResult(in pIndex as Integer) returns Array
   variable tBarcode as Array
   variable tP as Pointer
   variable tN as Integer
   put {} into tBarcode
   unsafe
      put _result_text(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["text"]
   unsafe
      put _result_format(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["format"]
   unsafe
      put _result_content_type(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["contentType"]
   unsafe
      put _result_symbology_id(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["symbologyId"]
   unsafe
      put _result_position(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["position"]
   unsafe
      put _result_extra(pIndex, "ECLevel") into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["ecLevel"]
   unsafe
      put _result_extra(pIndex, "Version") into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["version"]
   unsafe
      put _result_extra(pIndex, "") into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["extra"]
   unsafe
      put _result_error(pIndex) into tP
   end unsafe
   put textOf(tP) into tBarcode["error"]
   unsafe
      put _result_bytes(pIndex) into tN
      put _bytes_pointer() into tP
   end unsafe
   put bytesOf(tP, tN) into tBarcode["bytes"]
   unsafe
      put _result_orientation(pIndex) into tN
   end unsafe
   put tN into tBarcode["orientation"]
   unsafe
      put _result_is_valid(pIndex) into tN
   end unsafe
   put tN is not 0 into tBarcode["valid"]
   unsafe
      put _result_is_mirrored(pIndex) into tN
   end unsafe
   put tN is not 0 into tBarcode["mirrored"]
   unsafe
      put _result_is_inverted(pIndex) into tN
   end unsafe
   put tN is not 0 into tBarcode["inverted"]
   return tBarcode
end handler

-- One read, for the public spellings: every barcode as an array, and the
-- shim's list released.
private handler readPicture(in pImageData as Data, in pWidth as Integer, in pHeight as Integer, in pFormats as String, in pOptions as String) returns List
   variable tFound as List
   variable tPixels as Pointer
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tIndex as Integer
   checkABI()
   put [] into tFound
   put pointerOf(pImageData) into tPixels
   unsafe
      put _read_image(tPixels, lengthOf(pImageData), pWidth, pHeight, pFormats, pOptions) into tCount
   end unsafe
   repeat with tIndex from 1 up to tCount
      push takeResult(tIndex) onto tFound
   end repeat
   unsafe
      _results_release()
   end unsafe
   return tFound
end handler

-- One create into the shim's symbol slot. True when a symbol is there.
private handler createSymbol(in pContent as Data, in pIsText as Boolean, in pFormat as String, in pOptions as String) returns Boolean
   variable tPointer as Pointer
   variable tMade as Integer
   checkABI()
   put pointerOf(pContent) into tPointer
   unsafe
      put _create_symbol(tPointer, lengthOf(pContent), asInt(pIsText), pFormat, pOptions) into tMade
   end unsafe
   return tMade is not 0
end handler

-- The symbol slot drawn as a picture array, and both slots released. Called
-- after createSymbol, whatever it returned (a refused create leaves nothing
-- to draw, and the array is empty).
private handler takeDrawing(in pMade as Boolean, in pScale as Integer, in pQuietZone as Boolean, in pShowText as Boolean) returns Array
   variable tPicture as Array
   variable tDrawn as Integer
   variable tLength as Integer
   variable tPixels as Pointer
   variable tWidth as Integer
   variable tHeight as Integer
   put {} into tPicture
   if pMade then
      unsafe
         put _render_image(pScale, asInt(pQuietZone), asInt(pShowText)) into tDrawn
      end unsafe
      if tDrawn is not 0 then
         unsafe
            put _render_width() into tWidth
            put _render_height() into tHeight
            put _render_pixels_length() into tLength
            put _render_pixels() into tPixels
         end unsafe
         put tWidth into tPicture["width"]
         put tHeight into tPicture["height"]
         put bytesOf(tPixels, tLength) into tPicture["imageData"]
      end if
   end if
   unsafe
      _created_release()
   end unsafe
   return tPicture
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- The library
-- =====================================================================

/**
Summary: Returns the ABI number of the native library that is loaded.

Returns: The ABI number; this binding needs 1.
*/
public handler bcABIVersion() returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

/**
Summary: Returns the versions of the native library and of zxing-cpp inside it.

Returns: Text such as "barcodext ABI 1; zxing-cpp 3.1.1".

Example:
put bcLibraryVersion() into field "status"

Description: A good first call. It throws if the library on disk has a
different ABI from this binding, which means the two come from different
releases.
*/
public handler bcLibraryVersion() returns String
   variable tP as Pointer
   checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _library_version() into tP
   end unsafe
   return textOf(tP)
end handler

/**
Summary: Returns why the last call was refused.

Returns: A sentence, or empty when the last call worked.

Example:
put bcCreate("hello", "EAN-13", 3) into tPicture
if tPicture is empty then answer bcLastError()

Description: No handler of this library throws on a bad picture or a bad
argument. A refused call returns empty and leaves its reason here. A read
that finds no barcode is not a refusal: its list is empty and so is this.
*/
public handler bcLastError() returns String
   variable tP as Pointer
   unsafe
      put _last_error() into tP
   end unsafe
   return textOf(tP)
end handler

/**
Summary: Returns the kinds of barcode this library can read.

Returns: The names, with commas between: "Code 39,Code 128,EAN-13,QR Code,..."

Example:
put bcFormats() into tKinds
replace comma with return in tKinds
put tKinds into field "kinds"

Description: A name from this list is what <bcRead> reports as a barcode's
"format" and what <bcReadFormats> takes to narrow a read. The list also
holds family names ("EAN/UPC", "DataBar") that stand for all their members.
*/
public handler bcFormats() returns String
   variable tP as Pointer
   checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _format_names(0) into tP
   end unsafe
   return textOf(tP)
end handler

/**
Summary: Returns the kinds of barcode this library can create.

Returns: The names, with commas between.

Example:
if "Data Matrix" is among the items of bcWritableFormats() then
   put bcCreate("part 4711", "Data Matrix", 6) into tPicture
end if

Description: A name from this list is what <bcCreate> takes as the kind
to make.
*/
public handler bcWritableFormats() returns String
   variable tP as Pointer
   checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _format_names(1) into tP
   end unsafe
   return textOf(tP)
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Reading
-- =====================================================================

/**
Summary: Finds every barcode in a picture.

pImageData: The picture's pixels: the imageData of an image object.
pWidth: The picture's width in pixels.
pHeight: The picture's height in pixels.

Returns: A list with one array for each barcode found. It is empty when
there is none, and when the call was refused (then <bcLastError> says why).

Example:
put bcRead(the imageData of image "photo", the width of image "photo", the height of image "photo") into tFound
repeat for each element tBarcode in tFound
   put tBarcode["format"] & ": " & tBarcode["text"] & return after field "log"
end repeat

Description: Looks for every kind of barcode, upright or turned, dark on
light or light on dark. Each barcode found is an array with these keys:

- "text": what the barcode says, as text.
- "format": its kind, such as "QR Code" or "EAN-13".
- "bytes": what the barcode holds as binary data, nothing decoded.
- "contentType": "Text", "Binary", "Mixed", "GS1", "ISO15434" or "UnknownECI".
- "position": its four corners in the picture, from the top left clockwise,
  as eight numbers "x1,y1,x2,y2,x3,y3,x4,y4".
- "orientation": how far it is turned, in degrees.
- "valid": true when it decoded without an error.
- "error": why it could not be trusted; empty for a good one.
- "mirrored" and "inverted": true when it was read as a mirror image, or
  light on dark.
- "symbologyId": the standard's identifier for the kind, such as "]Q1".
- "ecLevel" and "version": a 2D code's error-correction level and size.
- "extra": every further fact the reader has, as a JSON object in text.

The pixels must be exactly pWidth * pHeight * 4 bytes, which is what an
image object's imageData is. To look only for some kinds (faster, and it
avoids a wrong guess) use <bcReadFormats>.
*/
public handler bcRead(in pImageData as Data, in pWidth as Integer, in pHeight as Integer) returns List
   return readPicture(pImageData, pWidth, pHeight, "", "")
end handler

/**
Summary: Finds the barcodes of the kinds you name in a picture.

pImageData: The picture's pixels: the imageData of an image object.
pWidth: The picture's width in pixels.
pHeight: The picture's height in pixels.
pFormats: The kinds to look for, with commas between, such as "QR Code" or "EAN-13,UPC-A". Empty looks for every kind.

Returns: A list with one array for each barcode found, as <bcRead> returns.

Example:
put bcReadFormats(the imageData of image "ticket", 640, 480, "QR Code,Aztec") into tFound
if the number of elements in tFound is 0 then answer "No ticket code found."

Description: Naming the kinds makes a read faster and stops a picture's
texture being taken for a kind you never use. The names are those of
<bcFormats>; "qrcode" and "ean13" spell the same kinds. A name the library
does not know refuses the call.

Some kinds are only told apart when asked for: a UPC-A is reported as an
EAN-13 with a leading 0 unless pFormats names "UPC-A".
*/
public handler bcReadFormats(in pImageData as Data, in pWidth as Integer, in pHeight as Integer, in pFormats as String) returns List
   return readPicture(pImageData, pWidth, pHeight, pFormats, "")
end handler

/**
Summary: Finds barcodes in a picture, with every choice the reader has.

pImageData: The picture's pixels: the imageData of an image object, or with the option "gray" one byte for each pixel.
pWidth: The picture's width in pixels.
pHeight: The picture's height in pixels.
pFormats: The kinds to look for, with commas between. Empty looks for every kind.
pOptions: Read options, with commas between. Empty uses the thorough defaults.

Returns: A list with one array for each barcode found, as <bcRead> returns.

Example:
-- a clean barcode this app drew itself: read it the fast way
put bcReadAdvanced(tPixels, tWidth, tHeight, "Code 128", "pure") into tFound

Example:
-- every frame of a camera: fast, one barcode is enough
put bcReadAdvanced(tPixels, tWidth, tHeight, "QR Code", "fast,max=1") into tFound

Description: The options are words with commas between, in any case:

- "fast": spend less time; a blurred or tiny barcode may be missed.
- "noRotate": do not look for barcodes turned on their side.
- "noInvert": do not look for light-on-dark barcodes.
- "noDownscale": do not also try the picture scaled down.
- "pure": the picture is a clean, upright, computer-made barcode and
  nothing else.
- "max=N": stop after N barcodes (1 to 255).
- "lines=N": a linear barcode must be seen on N scan lines (1 to 20).
- "errors": also return barcodes that were found but failed their check,
  with "valid" false and "error" saying why.
- "checksum": check the optional check digit of Code 39 and ITF.
- "addon=ignore", "addon=read" or "addon=require": what to do with the
  small 2 or 5 digit add-on beside an EAN or UPC barcode.
- "text=hri", "text=plain", "text=eci", "text=escaped" or "text=hex": how
  "text" is written. "hri" (the default) is what a person reads under the
  bars; "hex" is the bytes as hex pairs.
- "binarizer=local", "binarizer=global", "binarizer=fixed" or
  "binarizer=bool": how grey becomes black and white. "local" (the
  default) copes with uneven light.
- "gray": pImageData is one byte for each pixel (0 black to 255 white),
  not imageData.

A word the reader does not know refuses the call, and <bcLastError> names
it.
*/
public handler bcReadAdvanced(in pImageData as Data, in pWidth as Integer, in pHeight as Integer, in pFormats as String, in pOptions as String) returns List
   return readPicture(pImageData, pWidth, pHeight, pFormats, pOptions)
end handler

/**
Summary: Returns the text of the first barcode found in a picture.

pImageData: The picture's pixels: the imageData of an image object.
pWidth: The picture's width in pixels.
pHeight: The picture's height in pixels.

Returns: The barcode's text, or empty when no barcode was found.

Example:
put bcReadText(the imageData of image "photo", the width of image "photo", the height of image "photo") into field "scanned"

Description: The shortest way to scan one barcode. It looks for every
kind and returns only the text of one. Use <bcRead> to learn the kind, to
get several barcodes, or to get binary content.
*/
public handler bcReadText(in pImageData as Data, in pWidth as Integer, in pHeight as Integer) returns String
   variable tFound as List
   variable tBarcode as Array
   put readPicture(pImageData, pWidth, pHeight, "", "max=1") into tFound
   if the number of elements in tFound is 0 then
      return ""
   end if
   put tFound[1] into tBarcode
   return tBarcode["text"]
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Creating
-- =====================================================================

/**
Summary: Makes a barcode as a picture.

pText: What the barcode should say.
pFormat: The kind to make, such as "QR Code", "EAN-13" or "Code 128".
pScale: The size of the smallest bar or square in pixels, from 1 to 64.

Returns: An array with the keys "width", "height" and "imageData", or an
empty array when the call was refused (then <bcLastError> says why).

Example:
put bcCreate("https://openxtalk.org/", "QR Code", 6) into tPicture
create image "code"
set the width of image "code" to tPicture["width"]
set the height of image "code" to tPicture["height"]
set the imageData of image "code" to tPicture["imageData"]

Description: The picture is black on white with the empty margin the kind
needs to be read (its quiet zone). Its size follows from the content and
pScale; set the image object to that size before setting its imageData.

Each kind has rules about what it can say: an EAN-13 is 12 or 13 digits, a
Code 39 has capitals and digits, a QR Code takes any text. Content a kind
cannot hold refuses the call. The kinds are those of <bcWritableFormats>.
*/
public handler bcCreate(in pText as String, in pFormat as String, in pScale as Integer) returns Array
   variable tMade as Boolean
   put createSymbol(utf8Of(pText), true, pFormat, "") into tMade
   return takeDrawing(tMade, pScale, true, false)
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a barcode as a picture, with every choice the writer has.

pText: What the barcode should say.
pFormat: The kind to make, such as "QR Code", "EAN-13" or "Code 128".
pOptions: Create options, with commas between. Empty uses the defaults.
pScale: The size of the smallest bar or square in pixels, from 1 to 64.
pQuietZone: True to leave the empty margin the kind needs around it.
pShowText: True to print the content under a linear barcode.

Returns: An array with the keys "width", "height" and "imageData", or an
empty array when the call was refused.

Example:
-- a QR code that survives a logo over its middle
put bcCreateAdvanced(tLink, "QR Code", "ecLevel=H", 8, true, false) into tPicture

Example:
-- a product barcode with its digits printed under it
put bcCreateAdvanced("5901234123457", "EAN-13", "", 3, true, true) into tPicture

Description: The create options are words with commas between:

- "ecLevel=...": how much damage the code survives. For a QR Code "L",
  "M", "Q" or "H" (most); other kinds take a number or a percentage.
- "version=N": the size of a 2D code, by its standard's numbering.
- "dataMask=N": a QR Code's mask pattern.
- "columns=N" and "rows=N": the shape of a PDF417 or a stacked DataBar.
- "eci=...": the character set to declare, such as "eci=UTF-8".
- "gs1": the content is GS1 data, such as "(01)09501101530003".
- "forceSquare": only square Data Matrix sizes.
- "readerInit": mark the code as one that programs a scanner.

A word the writer does not know, or a value a kind does not accept,
refuses the call and <bcLastError> says why.
*/
public handler bcCreateAdvanced(in pText as String, in pFormat as String, in pOptions as String, in pScale as Integer, in pQuietZone as Boolean, in pShowText as Boolean) returns Array
   variable tMade as Boolean
   put createSymbol(utf8Of(pText), true, pFormat, pOptions) into tMade
   return takeDrawing(tMade, pScale, pQuietZone, pShowText)
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a barcode that holds binary data, as a picture.

pData: The bytes the barcode should hold.
pFormat: The kind to make. Only some kinds hold binary data: QR Code, Data Matrix, Aztec and PDF417 do.
pOptions: Create options, with commas between, as <bcCreateAdvanced> takes. Empty uses the defaults.
pScale: The size of the smallest bar or square in pixels, from 1 to 64.

Returns: An array with the keys "width", "height" and "imageData", or an
empty array when the call was refused.

Example:
put bcCreateFromData(tKeyBytes, "QR Code", "", 6) into tPicture

Description: The bytes are stored as they are, with no character set
applied. Read them back from the "bytes" key of what <bcRead> returns.
*/
public handler bcCreateFromData(in pData as Data, in pFormat as String, in pOptions as String, in pScale as Integer) returns Array
   variable tMade as Boolean
   put createSymbol(pData, false, pFormat, pOptions) into tMade
   return takeDrawing(tMade, pScale, true, false)
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a barcode as SVG text.

pText: What the barcode should say.
pFormat: The kind to make, such as "QR Code" or "EAN-13".
pOptions: Create options, with commas between, as <bcCreateAdvanced> takes. Empty uses the defaults.
pShowText: True to print the content under a linear barcode.

Returns: An SVG document as text, or empty when the call was refused.

Example:
put bcCreateSVG("5901234123457", "EAN-13", "", true) into url ("file:" & tFolder & "/label.svg")

Description: SVG is a drawing, not pixels, so it prints sharp at any
size. Use it for labels and documents; use <bcCreate> for a picture to
show in a stack.
*/
public handler bcCreateSVG(in pText as String, in pFormat as String, in pOptions as String, in pShowText as Boolean) returns String
   variable tMade as Boolean
   variable tP as Pointer
   variable tSvg as String
   put createSymbol(utf8Of(pText), true, pFormat, pOptions) into tMade
   put "" into tSvg
   if tMade then
      unsafe
         put _svg_text(1, 1, asInt(pShowText)) into tP
      end unsafe
      put textOf(tP) into tSvg
   end if
   unsafe
      _created_release()
   end unsafe
   return tSvg
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a barcode as rows of 1 and 0, for a stack that draws its own.

pText: What the barcode should say.
pFormat: The kind to make, such as "QR Code".
pOptions: Create options, with commas between, as <bcCreateAdvanced> takes. Empty uses the defaults.

Returns: Text with one line for each row of the barcode, "1" for a dark
square and "0" for a light one, or empty when the call was refused.

Example:
put bcCreateMatrix("hello", "QR Code", "") into tRows
put the number of lines in tRows into tSize -- 21 for the smallest QR Code

Description: There is no margin around the rows: leave the kind's quiet
zone yourself when you draw them (four squares for a QR Code). A linear
barcode comes back as many identical rows.
*/
public handler bcCreateMatrix(in pText as String, in pFormat as String, in pOptions as String) returns String
   variable tMade as Boolean
   variable tP as Pointer
   variable tRows as String
   put createSymbol(utf8Of(pText), true, pFormat, pOptions) into tMade
   put "" into tRows
   if tMade then
      unsafe
         put _modules_text() into tP
      end unsafe
      put textOf(tP) into tRows
   end if
   unsafe
      _created_release()
   end unsafe
   return tRows
end handler

end library
